<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Apis on STEP Batch 12</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/tags/apis/</link><description>Recent content in Apis on STEP Batch 12</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en_US</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:19:42 +0530</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://step-12.github.io/tags/apis/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>String APIs</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/string_apis/</link><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:10:00 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/intro_js/string_apis/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="working-with-strings"&gt;Working with Strings&lt;a class="anchor" href="#working-with-strings"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;ve learned that strings are another kind of value in JavaScript.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We can write strings:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Hello World&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;We can combine them:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Hello&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34; World&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;And we can put expressions inside them using template strings:&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-js" data-lang="js"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;&amp;#34;Anu&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Hello &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;So far, we&amp;rsquo;ve mostly treated strings as complete pieces of text.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;But what happens when we want to work with only a part of a string?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;What if we want to check whether a string contains a particular word?&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>